---
title: Competing risks
guide-section: "Multiple events"
bread-crumbs: false
---



So far every method has assumed a single kind of event. Often that is not realistic. A patient with
a precancerous condition might progress to cancer, but they might also die of something else first,
and death from another cause prevents the progression from ever being observed. These are competing
risks: mutually exclusive event types where the occurrence of one removes the possibility of the
others. Analyzing them with ordinary survival methods, one event type at a time, gives biased and
even nonsensical results. This page explains why, and shows the two standard tools Greenwood
provides.

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-competing
%%| fig-cap: "A competing-risks model: from an initial state, a subject can move to exactly one of several absorbing event states."
flowchart LR
    A[Event-free] --> B[Progression]
    A --> C[Death]
```

We use the bundled `mgus2` dataset, which follows patients with monoclonal gammopathy. The competing
events are progression to a plasma-cell malignancy and death without progression. The dataset
records each endpoint in its own pair of columns: `ptime` and `pstat` for progression, and `futime`
and `death` for death. A competing-risks analysis needs one time and one cause per subject, and
`gw.Surv.first_event()` builds exactly that from the pairs.

::: {#5d47478a .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import greenwood as gw

# Load the mgus2 dataset: monoclonal-gammopathy patients with competing-risk follow-up
mg = gw.load_dataset("mgus2", backend="polars")

# Name each endpoint's (time, event) columns, and the first observed endpoint wins
y = gw.Surv.first_event(
    endpoints={"pcm": ("ptime", "pstat"), "death": ("futime", "death")},
    data=mg,
)
y
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
Surv(type=right, n=1384, events=975, states=('pcm', 'death'))
```
:::
:::


For each subject, the endpoint with the earliest observed event becomes the cause, and its time
becomes the event time. Because a subject can experience only the first of the competing events,
one time per subject is all we need. Ties go to the endpoint listed first, so a progression
diagnosed at the same visit as death counts as progression. A subject with no observed event is
censored at the latest of the endpoint times, the last time they were known to be event-free.
Here are the first several subjects, with the times in months.

::: {#da15739f .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Show each subject's event time and state
y.to_frame(format="polars").head(8)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
