## Testing for group differences with Gray's test

The Aalen-Johansen estimator and stratified CIF show the cumulative incidence curves by group, but
do not formally test whether the groups differ. Gray's test (1988) is the competing-risks analogue
of the log-rank test. It compares the cumulative incidence function for a specific cause across
groups, properly accounting for competing events.

::: {#aeb15b45 .cell execution_count=11}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Rebuild the dataset and response
mg = gw.load_dataset("mgus2", backend="polars")

y = gw.Surv.first_event(
    endpoints={"pcm": ("ptime", "pstat"), "death": ("futime", "death")},
    data=mg,
)

# Test whether the CIF of pcm differs between sexes
gw.grays_test(y, group="sex", data=mg, cause="pcm")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=11}
```
TestResult(method="Gray's test (cause='pcm')", statistic=1.1945, df=1, p_value=0.2744)
```
:::
:::


The test returns a `TestResult` with the chi-square statistic, degrees of freedom, p-value, and
per-group observed vs. expected event counts. You can test any cause in the competing-risks response
by changing the `cause=` argument.

::: {#f8aaaa4f .cell execution_count=12}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Test the death cause instead
gw.grays_test(y, group="sex", cause="death", data=mg)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=12}
```
TestResult(method="Gray's test (cause='death')", statistic=11.6513, df=1, p_value=0.0006416)
```
:::
:::


Gray's test uses the subdistribution risk set, the same framework behind the Fine-Gray model.
Subjects who experience a competing event remain in the risk set with inverse-probability-of-
censoring weights. This makes the test sensitive to differences in the cumulative incidence function
itself, not just the cause-specific hazard rate. For comparing more than two groups, the test
produces a multi-degree-of-freedom chi-square statistic.

::: {.callout-note}
## Gray's test vs. the log-rank test

The log-rank test applied to cause-specific hazards asks "do the cause-specific hazard rates
differ?". Gray's test asks "do the cumulative incidence functions differ?". These questions can give
different answers because the CIF depends on all cause-specific hazards, not just the target one.
:::

## Modeling with the Fine-Gray model

The cumulative incidence function describes groups. To model how covariates affect the incidence of
a specific cause, use the Fine-Gray model. It is a regression on the subdistribution hazard,
constructed so that its coefficients describe the effect of covariates on the cumulative incidence
itself, which is usually what you want to report.

::: {#93ffd2db .cell execution_count=13}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Rebuild the dataset and response
mg = gw.load_dataset("mgus2", backend="polars")

y = gw.Surv.first_event(
    endpoints={"pcm": ("ptime", "pstat"), "death": ("futime", "death")},
    data=mg,
)

# Fit the Fine-Gray model for the pcm cause with age and sex as covariates
fg = gw.FineGray(cause="pcm").fit(y, covariates=["age", "sex"], data=mg)
fg
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=13}
```
FineGray (Fine-Gray subdistribution hazard model, cause='pcm')

         coef  exp(coef)  se(coef)       z         p
age   -0.0173     0.9828  0.005701  -3.035  0.002408
sexM  -0.2597     0.7713    0.1856  -1.399    0.1617

n = 1384, events = 115
Standard errors: robust (clustered)
```
:::
:::


We name `pcm` as the target cause and fit against `age` and `sex`. To read the fitted model we tidy
it into a coefficient table, asking for exponentiated estimates so the numbers are on the
hazard-ratio scale rather than the log scale.

::: {#3090635c .cell execution_count=14}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Exponentiate to get subdistribution hazard ratios
gw.tidy(fg, exponentiate=True, format="polars")
```

