This is the standard analysis layout in competing-risks studies: one Fine-Gray model (for cumulative
incidence and prediction) and one or two cause-specific Cox models (for etiological insight). When
the two model types disagree on the direction of an effect, both are usually reported because they
answer different questions.

### Predicting cumulative incidence from a Fine-Gray model

Fitting a Fine-Gray model tells you which covariates matter, but the clinical question is often
"what is this patient's predicted probability of experiencing the event by time $t$?" The
`predict_cumulative_incidence()` method answers that. It uses the baseline subdistribution
cumulative hazard from the training data together with the fitted coefficients to compute
$F(t \mid x) = 1 - \exp\bigl(-H_0(t)\,\exp(x\beta)\bigr)$ for each subject.

::: {#dc8242ef .cell execution_count=19}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Rebuild the dataset and fit the Fine-Gray model
mg = gw.load_dataset("mgus2", backend="polars")

y = gw.Surv.first_event(
    endpoints={"pcm": ("ptime", "pstat"), "death": ("futime", "death")},
    data=mg,
)

fg = gw.FineGray(cause="pcm").fit(y, covariates=["age", "sex"], data=mg)

# Predict cumulative incidence at 10, 20, and 30 years for three subjects
fg.predict_cumulative_incidence(mg[:3], times=[120, 240, 360], format="polars")
```

