Each column is one subject and each row is a time point. The values are the predicted probability of
PCM progression by that time, accounting for the competing risk of death. A subject with higher risk
covariates accumulates incidence faster.

You can also call `predict()` for the raw linear predictor or relative subdistribution hazard, which
are useful for model evaluation with `concordance_index_incidence()` and `brier_score_incidence()`.

::: {#60eeba44 .cell execution_count=20}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Linear predictor (centered) for the first five subjects
fg.predict(mg[:5], type="lp")
```

