## Penalized Fine-Gray for high-dimensional covariates

When the number of covariates is large relative to the number of target-cause events, the
unpenalized Fine-Gray model may overfit or fail to converge. `PenalizedFineGray` adds an elastic-net
penalty to the subdistribution-hazard partial likelihood, enabling variable selection and
coefficient shrinkage. It is the competing-risks analogue of `CoxNet`.

The penalty is $\lambda\bigl(\alpha\|\beta\|_1 + \tfrac{1-\alpha}{2}\|\beta\|_2^2\bigr)$, where
`penalizer` is $\lambda$ and `l1_ratio` is $\alpha$. Setting `l1_ratio=1` gives lasso (sparse),
`l1_ratio=0` gives ridge (smooth shrinkage), and values in between blend the two.

::: {#c79f308f .cell execution_count=21}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Rebuild the dataset and response
mg = gw.load_dataset("mgus2", backend="polars")

y = gw.Surv.first_event(
    endpoints={"pcm": ("ptime", "pstat"), "death": ("futime", "death")},
    data=mg,
)

# Fit a lasso-penalized Fine-Gray model
pfg = gw.PenalizedFineGray(cause="pcm", penalizer=0.01, l1_ratio=1.0).fit(
    y, covariates=["age", "sex"], data=mg
)
pfg
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=21}
```
PenalizedFineGray (elastic-net Fine-Gray, cause='pcm', penalizer=0.01, l1_ratio=1.0)

           coef
age   -0.008028
sexM  -0.002464

n = 1384, events = 115, nonzero coefficients = 2
```
:::
:::


The fitted object shows the penalized coefficients and how many survived shrinkage. Just like the
unpenalized `FineGray`, you can predict cumulative incidence for new subjects.

::: {#0c9ea587 .cell execution_count=22}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Predict cumulative incidence at landmark times
pfg.predict_cumulative_incidence(mg[:3], times=[120, 240, 360], format="polars")
```

