### Choosing the penalty strength

A stronger penalty shrinks more coefficients toward (or exactly to) zero, improving generalizability
at the cost of bias. To find the right balance, fit models at several penalty values and evaluate
with `concordance_index_incidence()` or `brier_score_incidence()` on held-out data.

::: {#9a9e73db .cell execution_count=24}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import numpy as np

# Compare penalized models at different strengths
for lam in [0.001, 0.01, 0.1]:
    m = gw.PenalizedFineGray(cause="pcm", penalizer=lam, l1_ratio=1.0).fit(
        y, covariates=["age", "sex"], data=mg
    )
    n_nz = int(np.count_nonzero(m.coef_))
    print(f"  penalizer={lam:.3f}  nonzero={n_nz}  coef={np.round(m.coef_, 4)}")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
