::: {.callout-tip}
## Ridge vs. lasso for competing risks

Use lasso (`l1_ratio=1`) when you want automatic variable selection, driving irrelevant coefficients
to exactly zero. Use ridge (`l1_ratio=0`) when all covariates are expected to contribute but you
want to stabilize estimates against multicollinearity. Elastic net (`0 < l1_ratio < 1`) blends both
behaviors.
:::

## Competing-risk random forest

The parametric and semiparametric models above all assume log-linear covariate effects. When the
relationship between covariates and cumulative incidence is non-linear or involves interactions, a
tree-based model can capture these patterns automatically.

`CompetingRiskForest` grows an ensemble of survival trees that split nodes using the log-rank CR
statistic. Unlike a standard random survival forest, the splitting criterion uses a Lau-inclusive
risk set: subjects who experienced a competing event remain at risk under the subdistribution
framework, so each split directly targets separation of the cumulative incidence functions. At each
leaf the Aalen-Johansen CIF is estimated for every cause, and the forest prediction averages these
across all trees.

::: {#b4706a73 .cell execution_count=25}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Fit a competing-risk forest
crf = gw.CompetingRiskForest(
    n_estimators=100, max_depth=5, oob_score=True, random_state=0
).fit(y, covariates=["age", "sex"], data=mg)
crf
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=25}
```
CompetingRiskForest (100 logrankCR trees, max_features='sqrt')
n = 1384, events = 975, features = 2
causes: pcm, death
out-of-bag concordance = 0.4511
```
:::
:::


Predict the cumulative incidence for a specific cause.

::: {#d98962f8 .cell execution_count=26}
